      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Francisville%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "On June 12, 1863, Union and Confederate Freemasons negotiated a truce to hold a joint funeral, an event later called \"The Day the War Stopped\" in St. Francisville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/St._Francisville,_Louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "St. Francisville hosts two preserved antebellum plantations—Rosedown State Historic Site and Audubon State Historic Site, where naturalist John James Audubon once worked.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.exploresouthernhistory.com/stfrancisville.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Myrtles plantation in St. Francisville is reputed to be the most haunted house in America, drawing curious visitors.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.exploresouthernhistory.com/stfrancisville.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Recent community initiatives in St. Francisville focus on restoring and preserving the town’s historic homes, keeping its architectural heritage alive.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
